## Why This Is Asked

This question probes multi-tenant privacy guarantees, real-time streaming accuracy, and admin-driven policy propagation—crucial for platforms handling sensitive audits.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation and access control
- Streaming backfill and latency budgets
- Admin-driven privacy filters and dynamic policy rollout
- Failure modes: Kafka outage, schema drift, topic compaction, replay semantics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify tenant isolation in REST and Kafka stream
const tenant = 'tenantA';
const consumer = kafka.consumer({ groupId: 'audit-tenantA' });
await consumer.subscribe({ topic: 'audits', fromBeginning: true });
let received = [];
for await (const msg of consumer.consume()) {
  const ev = JSON.parse(msg.value.toString());
  if (ev.tenantId !== tenant) throw new Error('cross-tenant event detected');
  received.push(ev);
  if (received.length >= 5) break;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify end-to-end latency from REST GET to stream emission?
- How would you test admin-flag propagation under high churn and network partitions?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end API testing in an event-driven pipeline, focusing on idempotency, schema versioning across tenants, and concurrency safety — realistic in multi-tenant ingestion workloads.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent ingestion and deduplication across HTTP and Kafka
- Tenant-scoped schema versions and overrides
- End-to-end visibility with distributed tracing
- Race conditions and replay handling in high concurrency
- Tooling: Testcontainers, Pact, k6, Postman

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: idempotent ingestion using idempotency-key header
async function testIdempotentIngest(client) {
  const payload = {tenant: 't1', version: 2, data: {a:1}};
  const key = 'abc-123';
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  await client.post('/ingest', payload, {headers: {'Idempotency-Key': key}});
  // verify only single consume
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect exactly-once vs at-least-once processing in the consumer?
- How would you test schema evolution across tenants without breaking data?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design end-to-end API tests for dynamic feature flags and multi-region consistency under outages.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed config propagation latency
- Per-tenant isolation and cache invalidation
- Canary rollouts and rollback safety
- Observability and chaos testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify flag flip propagates across regions within TTL
async function testFlagPropagation(regions, tenantId, flagKey, expected) {
  const before = await fetchRecommendations(tenantId, regions[0]);
  await flipFlag(flagKey, true); // in etcd
  for (const r of regions) {
    const v = await pollUntilChanged(() => fetchRecommendations(tenantId, r), old => old !== before, 60000);
    if (v === before) throw new Error('Flag did not propagate in region ' + r);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure TTL impact on user-perceived latency during flag flips?
- How would you test partial outages where some regions see new flags while others don't?

